# Implementation notes

The implementation uses only Go's standard library. `main.go` implements the
JSON-lines interface, request validation, and identification. `wire.go` implements
transaction encoding and decoding. `main_test.go` contains independently laid out
wire examples, edge cases, randomized round trips, and fuzz targets.

Build with `go build -o rosettanode .`; run with `./rosettanode`. Test with
`go test ./...`. No dependency downloads are required. Building with
`GO111MODULE=off` also works. Responses are flushed after each input line, and a
final line without a newline is processed. Empty lines produce `invalid_request`.

## Interpretations of unspecified behavior

`interface.md` supplies the JSON shapes but does not specify a binary grammar,
validation policy, default limits, or error precedence. The implementation makes
these choices:

- Transactions use conventional Bitcoin serialization: little-endian fixed-width
  fields and canonical CompactSize lengths/counts. Decoding rejects nonminimal
  CompactSize values. No script execution or transaction consensus checks occur.
- Version bits, previous indices, sequences, and locktime are unsigned 32-bit
  values. Amounts are signed 64-bit values, encoded as little-endian two's
  complement. Offsets and `max_items` are unsigned 64-bit values. Decimal strings
  contain digits, with an optional minus sign for amounts; leading zeros are accepted.
  Hexadecimal accepts either case, requires whole bytes, and has no `0x` prefix.
  Output numbers and hex strings are normalized.
- Previous transaction digests are exactly 32 bytes and appear on the wire in
  the supplied digest order. Identifiers use double SHA256; display order reverses
  the resulting 32 digest bytes. The full identifier equals the stripped
  identifier when there is no witness.
- Witness mode also accepts legacy transactions. Zero initial inputs followed by
  a nonzero byte indicate witness framing. Only flag `01` is supported. Marker
  and flag `00 00` represent the legacy empty-input, empty-output form. Legacy
  mode never interprets a witness marker specially.
- Witness framing is emitted only when `include_witness` is true and at least one
  input has a nonempty witness stack. A stack containing one empty item counts
  as present. Framing with all empty stacks is rejected during decoding. Empty
  witness stacks are emitted for other inputs when any input has witness data.
- Prefix offsets count bytes, and `consumed` is the number of transaction bytes
  read starting at that offset. Out-of-range offsets are `invalid_request`;
  insufficient bytes at an in-range offset are `malformed_encoding`.
  Exact decoding rejects trailing bytes as `malformed_encoding`.
- Absent `max_items` means no explicit count cap. A supplied cap applies separately
  to input/output counts, witness stack counts, and every script/witness byte
  string length. It is not an aggregate budget and does not count fixed-width
  digests. Transaction requests validate witness limits even when serializing
  without witness. Limit excess is `resource_limit`; an unrepresentable encoded
  size is also `resource_limit`.
- Every listed transaction field is required, including witness arrays; null is
  not a substitute for an empty array, string, or object. `include_witness` is
  required for serialization, and `offset` is required for prefix decoding.
  Unknown JSON properties are ignored. Duplicate keys follow Go's JSON decoder
  behavior: the last value is used.
- Invalid JSON, request shapes, hex, numeric values, modes, or operations produce
  `invalid_request`. Binary truncation and invalid wire encodings produce
  `malformed_encoding`. Canonicality and minimum available payload size are
  checked before a decoded collection's limit; the limit is then checked before
  consuming or allocating its payload. There is no global precedence guarantee
  when multiple independent faults coexist.
- No arbitrary transport or admission thresholds are introduced because none
  were supplied. `transport_limit` and `admission_limit` are therefore not emitted.
  Unexpected recoverable implementation panics produce `execution_failure`.

## Validation

Tests cover fixed legacy/witness bytes, identifiers and digest reversal, empty
transactions, mixed witness stacks, numeric boundaries, malformed request shapes,
all truncations of the fixed transactions, canonical CompactSize boundaries,
oversized declared counts, per-collection limits, prefix offsets, exact trailing
bytes, 252/253/65535/65536-byte payloads, 253-element collections, 300 deterministic
randomized transaction round trips, and multiline transport including large and
unterminated records. Fuzz targets check binary decoding and JSON request handling.

## Repair phase

The supplied `feedback.json` resolved amount signedness and exposed a case where
a truncated input collection was incorrectly classified as `resource_limit`.
Amounts now use the signed 64-bit range throughout parsing, encoding, decoding,
and identification. Collection checks now test physical feasibility before the
configured limit. Applying that precedence to all collection types is a
generalization of the supplied input-count case; full error precedence remains
unspecified.

`feedback_test.go` embeds the six supplied examples for exact response regression
checks, and adds signed boundary/range tests and paired truncated/complete
collection tests. Feedback is included only in the test executable, never the
JSON-lines program.
